<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dore, South Yorkshire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dore is a former village, now a suburb of Sheffield, which lay on the medieval boundary between Mercia and Northumbria; its name means a pass or door. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a>, in 829 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egbert-of-wessex">Ecgberht of Wessex</a>, having conquered Mercia, led his army to Dore, where the Northumbrians submitted to him, though his overlordship in the north proved brief.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dore-south-yorkshire.jpg" alt="Dore, South Yorkshire" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AndyScott · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dore,_Christ_Church_from_the_churchyard_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
